VRTOS News - History of changes visible to users.

Version 0.6.0 (Beta Release)
=========================================
We are getting closer to the stable release!
This version brings a massive architectural leap to AVRTOS, turning it into a truly preemptive and stable Real-Time Operating System. The major highlights include:

*Preemptive Timer-Based Scheduling*: The time-slice mechanism has been completely replaced with a robust AVR timer-based preemptive scheduler. The scheduler logic is now fairer and prioritizes selection speed over CPU holding time.
*Atomic VM Operations & Safe Preemption*: Implemented atomic instruction execution and critical section tracking using interrupt disabling. This ensures system stability during preemptive context switches and solves major race conditions.
*Non-Blocking EEPROM Syscalls*: Introduced a non-blocking EEPROM write mechanism via system calls, completely eliminating timing jitter caused by long write cycles.
*Process Sleep & Wait States*: Added a new system call to allow processes to sleep for specific durations, and upgraded the scheduler to natively handle sleeping and waiting states efficiently.
*Task Creation via Syscall*: Creating new tasks has been moved from being a VM bytecode instruction to a dedicated system call, standardizing the API.


Version 0.5.0 (Experimental "Alpha" Release)
=========================================
Here comes the first public version of AVRTOS after its hidden development period, and I am happy to announce it! Despite its experimental nature, this early version of the software, named 0.5.0, already features many exciting things which allow using advanced functionality in AVR microcontrollers, namely:

*Virtual Machine Like x86*: The whole virtual machine structure with four general-purpose registers, address registers, an execution stack, and all sorts of arithmetic and bitwise operations.
*Page Caching for EEPROM Code*: A clean structure that enables you to run code directly from EEPROM blocks without wasting all of AVR's scarce SRAM.
*Priority Scheduler*: A multitasking scheduler that can distinguish between "elites" and "lowly" processes and handle any kind of process starvation properly.
*Smart GPIO Watching (or PCINT)*: The effective use of Pin Change Interrupts, with special states `pinsleep`, where processes can wait until the given hardware pin reaches the required logic level.